      "writtenOn": "2026-04-16T02:07:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Just wondering: does QEMU or any other known VMM create such CPUID entries?\n\nQEMU doesn\u0027t do this as QEMU will set the CPUID00.EAX with a non-zero value according to the KVM supported CPUID, QEMU may also adjust according to the features it supported but doesn\u0027t set it 0.\n\nI know there are other VMMs, like cloud-hypervisor, firecracker, which are rust based VMM. Maybe it is not that important for those VMMs?\n\n\u003e...Generally, this feels pretty hacky, but I\u0027m probably fine with merging it, since I guess have no better solution right now, and this is just another indication that this whole approach with silently modifying the CPUID entries set by host is rather hacky, and we should eventually change it to let crosvm and pKVM cooperatively set a desired CPUID for pVMs.\n\nOTOH, even we have a desired CPUID for pVMs, there are still some empty CPUID entries in the CPUID memory pages as the entries may not be able to full fill a memory page. In this case, a desired CPUID should guarantee that the valid CPUID00H should sit before any other empty CPUID entries, so that `kvm_find_cpuid_entry2` can find out this valid CPUID00H before seeing any other empty CPUID entries? Otherwise it still hit the same problem (intend to get valid CPUID00H, but get an empty CPUID entry). Maybe we should enforce this right now for the pVM. Actually I think for the no-pKVM case, KVM already assume the valid CPUID00H sites before any other empty CPUID entries, otherwise it will also hit the same problem. So a better solution maybe ensuring the CPUID00H sitting before any other empty CPUID entries in the `pkvm_enforce_cpuid`, like this:\n\n```\n--- a/arch/x86/kvm/cpuid.c\n+++ b/arch/x86/kvm/cpuid.c\n@@ -2382,7 +2382,7 @@ static struct kvm_cpuid_entry2 *find_cpuid_entry(struct kvm_cpuid_entry2 *buf,\n int pkvm_enforce_cpuid(struct kvm_cpuid_entry2 *e2, int *nent, int max_nent)\n {\n        struct kvm_cpuid_entry2 *de2 \u003d this_cpu_ptr(cpuid_def);\n-       int def_nent, r, i, n;\n+       int def_nent, r, i, n, first_ee_index;\n        int orig_nent \u003d *nent;\n        bool has_func4 \u003d false;\n\n@@ -2401,12 +2401,18 @@ int pkvm_enforce_cpuid(struct kvm_cpuid_entry2 *e2, int *nent, int max_nent)\n        if (r)\n                return r;\n\n+       first_ee_index \u003d orig_nent;\n        /* Enforce cpuid leaves according to the default set */\n        for (i \u003d 0; i \u003c orig_nent; i++) {\n                struct kvm_cpuid_entry2 *tmp;\n\n-               if (cpuid_entry_is_empty(\u0026e2[i]) ||\n-                   pkvm_cpuid_entry_host_owned(\u0026e2[i]))\n+               if (cpuid_entry_is_empty(\u0026e2[i])) {\n+                       if (i \u003c first_ee_index)\n+                               first_ee_index \u003d i;\n+                       continue;\n+               }\n+\n+               if (pkvm_cpuid_entry_host_owned(\u0026e2[i]))\n                        continue;\n\n                if (e2[i].function \u003d\u003d 4) {\n@@ -2415,10 +2421,21 @@ int pkvm_enforce_cpuid(struct kvm_cpuid_entry2 *e2, int *nent, int max_nent)\n                }\n\n                tmp \u003d find_cpuid_entry(de2, def_nent, \u0026e2[i]);\n-               if (tmp)\n+               if (tmp) {\n+                       /*\n+                        * Function 0 should sit before the first empty CPUID\n+                        * entry (all zero entry) to guarantee pKVM can find\n+                        * out the valid CPUID00H via kvm_find_cpuid_entry2()\n+                        * which return the first entry whose function is zero.\n+                        */\n+                       if (e2[i].function \u003d\u003d 0 \u0026\u0026 i \u003e first_ee_index)\n+                               return -EINVAL;\n                        pkvm_enforce_cpuid_entry(\u0026e2[i], tmp);\n-               else\n+               } else {\n                        memset(\u0026e2[i], 0, sizeof(struct kvm_cpuid_entry2));\n+                       if (i \u003c first_ee_index)\n+                               first_ee_index \u003d i;\n+               }\n        }\n\n        /* Insert default cpuid leaves if missing in the host buffer */\n@@ -2448,6 +2465,15 @@ int pkvm_enforce_cpuid(struct kvm_cpuid_entry2 *e2, int *nent, int max_nent)\n                if (n \u003d\u003d max_nent)\n                        return -ENOSPC;\n\n+               /*\n+                * If insert CPUID00H, it should be sit at the first empty\n+                * CPUID entry to guarantee the pKVM can find out this CPUID00H\n+                * via kvm_find_cpuid_entry2() which return the first entry\n+                * whose function is zero.\n+                */\n+               if (e2[i].function \u003d\u003d 0 \u0026\u0026 n !\u003d first_ee_index)\n+                       return -EINVAL;\n+\n                e2[n++] \u003d de2[i];\n        }\n\n```\n\n\u003eBTW it seems we can change cpuid_entry_is_empty() to check if all 4 regs eax, ebx, ecx, edx are zero, not just eax? And thus easily avoid regressing such VMMs that set CPUID with max_standard_function\u003d0 but a non-empty vendor string?\n\nIf a malicious host places entries with unknown functions at lower buffer positions than the real function 0 entry, those entries become all-zero for 4 regs after enforcement. kvm_find_cpuid_entry2() then returns one of these zeroed entries for function 0 queries instead of the real one, causing the pVM to see max_standard_function\u003d0 and an empty vendor string. So seems checking all 4 regs in cpuid_entry_is_empty() doesn\u0027t help.",
      "parentUuid": "e6147e52_b9be5109",
      "revId": "34f8cf5350633d6c67e43f1ecb784da9b8f8712a",
